{
    "word": "coenobium",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                "word": "coenobium",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "17289fdd-8c9c-4f5c-84e6-4d9cacb6d4c3",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "coenobiums"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "d4062fca-b3e6-4bc4-8328-f2fefcf6a616",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "coenobia"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A conventual or monastic community; also, a convent or monastery."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ecdd4872-04d3-457e-b98a-6f4beb0f0258",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "[T]his profeſſion of a ſingle life vvas propagated in Egypt by Antony, and in Syria by Hilarion; and ſpred ſo faſt, that ſoon after the time of Julian the Apoſtate a third part of Egyptians vvere got into the deſarts of Egypt. They lived firſt ſingly in cells, then aſſociated into cœnobia or convents; and at length came into tovvns, and filled the Churches vvith Biſhops, Presbyters and Deacons."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0973e339-45be-418c-a2a8-d7fb19c4a451",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "Some carts are entirely filled with representatives of the Church, but these are jolly, portly fellows, who pay loyally, and hasten to secure the most delicate fare for the table of their different cœnobiums."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "14b7ed89-4271-4f40-8223-d84ef00c92f0",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "[A]fter I had passed thirty years living in a cœnobium, I rejoice that I have also spent twenty more, so that I can never be accused of sloth among those who tried it in a half-hearted way."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "78cd1586-7178-4e1c-8042-fe6a738b1dbd",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "There were the cenobia, or monasteries proper, where the life was according to the lines laid down by St Basil; and there were the lauras, wherein a semi-eremitical life was followed, the monks living in separate huts within the enclosure."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4e8cffd-db2e-4b65-bf20-ad52531dd04d",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "Sometimes it [the church] is called Martyrium, when raised in honour of any Martyr; somes capella, (chapel,) … sometimes caenobium, at others sacrificium; sometimes sacellum; sometimes the House of Prayer; sometimes monastery; sometimes oratory."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e0e6c4ac-43af-4e79-a4d6-1313bf4237a9",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "The Encomiast refers to St. Bertin's and St. Omer's as monasteria (II, 21, I and II) and caenobia (ibid., 15). […] The words monasterium and caenobium are, however, both freely used in medieval Latin in the sense 'collegiate church'."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f694ea1f-22af-4e61-96c4-3b3cd2411a2e",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "Psalmody refers to singing or reciting psalms; in the cenobium this was performed communally seven times a day."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "982bd353-e000-4866-a1ba-b735e0fc158e",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "It is no exaggeration to say that among these foundations, those of [Vincent of] Lérins and St. Victor of Marseilles were so outstanding that the Gallic monasticism of the 5ᵗʰ century has to be identified with the history of these two coenobiums, and only partly with that of the monastic settlement in the Jura."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6eafb4ad-79d1-4d48-8608-f4ecf32c6c11",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "We are part of a community, a cenobium, and we are under the spiritual direction of an abbot and his delegates."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e906b522-87d1-4af4-975a-15c508b25cc5",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "The cenobium is the proper locus for the acquisition of virtue and in the nineteenth conference he has the Abbot John [Cassian], who had passed thirty years in the cenobium, twenty as an anchorite and then returned to the cenobium, expound the dangers of the desert and the advantages of the cenobium."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "38453cfc-4abe-433d-a82d-628ee47f24f4",
                                "sense_id": "4e4f86be-0c36-3cf4-afa6-9de3b936938e",
                                "text": "An anagārika does not formally commit himself (or herself) to the strictly cenobitical monastic restraints of a caenobium or monastery, such as finishing one's begging round for food before midday, and not handling money."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ad492554-0fb1-3999-8060-9e461ceea0f7",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A fruit of a plant from either of the families Boraginaceae (the borages) or Lamiaceae which has small loculi or compartments, reminiscent of the cells in a convent or monastery."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d6ff3882-ac02-4a3f-847f-a7b707d63bff",
                                "sense_id": "ad492554-0fb1-3999-8060-9e461ceea0f7",
                                "text": "Comfrey fruit is a coenobium consisting not of two (in carpel number) but four monospermous eremes. Eremes are not opened and seed not released from pericarp falls from the plant."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "49069a26-4cad-3ac8-8c06-e5c5058870ea",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A colony of algae which acts as a single organism; a coenobe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9b3f5b3e-1b42-40f4-aebf-d5fa4e9d1be6",
                                "sense_id": "49069a26-4cad-3ac8-8c06-e5c5058870ea",
                                "text": "[…] M. [Louis-Félix] Henneguy finds four kinds of these colonies, which he calls cœnobiums: […] At the moment of fecundation the bundles of antherozoids are set at liberty by the dissolution of the antheridia wall; they move quickly through the water, and fix themselves on the female cœnobiums, and then separate to fecundate the oospheres, but the author was not able to observe the exact moment of their penetration."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "03038a51-5fef-4bd1-8109-337fcf9a759d",
                                "sense_id": "49069a26-4cad-3ac8-8c06-e5c5058870ea",
                                "text": "This group includes two genera, Volvox and Eudorina, which resemble Pandorina in many respects, and were formerly included with it in one group. Like Pandorina, these plants are motile, and consist of a number of ciliated cells aggregated into a cœnobium. […] The distinction between these plants and Pandorina is that in them certain cells of the cœnobium develops into antheridia and oogonia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dd22f993-7600-4f60-8e80-333b4866bb96",
                                "sense_id": "49069a26-4cad-3ac8-8c06-e5c5058870ea",
                                "text": "Genus, PEDIASTRUM, Meyen. Cells united into definite families, known as coenobiums; they are plane, discoid or stellate, swimming free. A coenobium is formed of cells in a single or rarely, in part, double stratum, which is continuous or perforated."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5f630669-cd81-433b-b533-b9a70a24f0b1",
                                "sense_id": "49069a26-4cad-3ac8-8c06-e5c5058870ea",
                                "text": "The study of ecological conditions under which the seasonal dynamics of meadow vegetation develops is one of [the] main parameters in exact studies of phytocenotic structure and the ecology of basic coenobiums.",
                                "translation": "Archives of Biological Sciences",
                                "english": "Archives of Biological Sciences"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f4660f66-2615-3889-a672-96b183704e3b",
                        "entry_id": "fe58181d-87ae-3d46-8054-c4053411443a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A colony of unicellular organisms (such as protozoa) which acts as a single organism."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "50e04887-9aa5-448c-b3e7-998b275ede70",
                                "sense_id": "f4660f66-2615-3889-a672-96b183704e3b",
                                "text": "The Radiolaria are marine rhizopods, whose unicellular body always consists of two parts,— […] The individuals are usually single: in only a small minority are the unicellular organisms united in colonies or caenobia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "77ce7bfb-36fb-4518-95a1-cb55707b47d3",
                                "sense_id": "f4660f66-2615-3889-a672-96b183704e3b",
                                "text": "eutely Phenomenon exhibited by a few phyla, notably nematodes, where all individuals have the same number of cells (or nuclei in a coenobium)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
